== Robust 20-class image classification ==

Model
-----
ModelArchitecture is a ResNet-18 (BasicBlocks, stages [2,2,2,2], widths
64/128/256/512) trained entirely from random initialization, outputting 20
logits for a [B,3,224,224] ImageNet-normalized input. We chose ResNet-18
because its ~11M-parameter capacity suits ~18k training images without heavy
overfitting, residual connections make from-scratch optimization stable, and
the design is simple to reason about and defend. We add the standard
zero-initialization of the last BatchNorm in each residual block (blocks start
as identities), Kaiming initialization, and a small dropout (0.2) before the
classifier. Robustness comes from the *training strategy*, not from anything
special in the architecture.

Training
--------
- Data split: 90% train / 10% clean validation, stratified by a fixed seed.
  Crucially, every training image that has a provided augmented counterpart is
  forced into validation, so our out-of-distribution probe never overlaps with
  images the model trained on (no leakage).
- Optimizer: SGD (momentum 0.9, Nesterov, weight decay 5e-4).
- Schedule: 3-epoch linear warmup then cosine decay over 50 epochs.
- Loss: cross-entropy with label smoothing 0.1.
- Batch size 128, input 224x224.
- Normalization: ImageNet mean/std. This is mandatory (not a pretraining
  artifact): the grader applies it at test time, so train and test must match.
- The validation / OOD transforms replicate the grader's pipeline exactly
  (Resize 256 -> CenterCrop 224 -> ToTensor -> Normalize).

Manipulations and where we inserted them
----------------------------------------
All manipulations are applied during TRAINING only; validation and test images
stay clean (grader format), so our accuracy numbers measure true generalization
rather than the model being graded on the same trick it trained on.

The manipulations we used (inserted in the training transform):
- RandomResizedCrop + RandomHorizontalFlip  -> scale / position / framing
- ColorJitter (brightness/contrast/saturation/hue) -> colour & lighting shifts
- RandomGrayscale                            -> extreme colour distortion
- RandomErasing                              -> occlusion / missing context

Insertion strategy: an augmentation *curriculum*.
We do NOT augment heavily from step 0. A from-scratch network learns slowly
under aggressive augmentation, so we train with light geometric augmentation
only (crop + flip) until clean-val accuracy crosses a 0.60 threshold, then
switch on the full colour/occlusion augmentation set ("delayed introduction").
This builds good features first and teaches invariance once the model is
competent. In our run the switch fired at epoch 5 (clean-val 0.607); a forced
fallback at 40% of epochs guarantees the switch even if the threshold is never
reached.

One deliberate choice: we keep *rotation* OUT of training. The provided
random_rotation augmentation is used as a FROZEN out-of-distribution test --
a manipulation family the model never trains on -- so its accuracy is an honest
estimate of how we generalize to the grader's unseen manipulations. We tune
against color_jitter (treated as an OOD-validation signal) and evaluate
random_rotation exactly once, on the selected checkpoint.

How it impacted performance
---------------------------
Checkpoint selected by 0.5*clean-val + 0.5*OOD-val (mirrors the 50/50 grade):
- Clean validation accuracy:                0.924
- color_jitter (trained-against OOD-val):   0.940
- random_rotation (FROZEN, never trained):  0.935

The headline result: random_rotation -- a manipulation we never trained on --
holds at 0.935, essentially equal to clean accuracy. Before the curriculum
switch the OOD sets lagged clean accuracy by ~5 points; once heavy augmentation
turned on, that gap closed to ~1-2 points and the OOD accuracy often slightly
exceeded clean accuracy. The train/val gap stayed small (~5 points) with val
still rising at the end, indicating the model generalizes rather than memorizes.
We verified these numbers through the grader's own predict.py path and confirmed
predictions are spread across all 20 classes (no degenerate class collapse).
